# STITCHV2

`stitchv2` is a Python/JAX implementation scaffold for a STITCH-style low-coverage imputation workflow with:

- streaming read-level `pysam` extraction over an explicit `(CHR, POS)` table
- founder panels from VCF, BAM-derived evidence, or PLINK via `npplink`
- block-wise Arrow/Parquet persistence
- JAX forward-backward kernels suitable for CPU/GPU execution
- optional pedigree-aware post-HMM imputation (`smooth`, `kinship`, or recombination-smoothed `transmission` message passing)
- fragment-coupled read likelihood support (multi-SNP read evidence, center-anchored)
- optional STITCH-parity centered fragment/read coupling (`fragment_coupling_model="stitch_parity"`)
- calibrated genotype posterior/call outputs (GP/GT parquet, optional xarray/Zarr float store, explicit BCF interoperability export)
- configurable ploidy (`--ploidy`, `--ploidy-males`, `--ploidy-females`) including sex-chromosome use cases
- optional chromosome-window runs (`--chr-start`, `--chr-end`)
- supporting-read mask output (`support_mask` parquet) aligned to sample x variant genotype outputs
- optional microarray hard-genotype integration from PLINK (`microarray_plink_path`)
- optional per-sample microarray PLINK sources via `samples.parquet: plink_path` (multiple samples can share files)
- 5-fold CV/tuning harness for K/nGen/S + seed averaging with optional LightGBM post-calibration

This package is designed for large cohorts where BAMs and posterior tensors must be processed in blocks and written to disk eagerly.

The implementation follows the published STITCH paper closely at the modeling level, but it is not a line-for-line port of the upstream R/C++ code. In a few places the paper is high level, so this package uses explicit documented approximations:

- recombination transitions use a Li-Stephens-style switch process parameterized by genetic distance and sample generation
- mutable founders are represented as per-founder alt-allele probabilities instead of hard haplotypes
- pedigree information can enter as post-HMM smoothing, kinship fallback, or parent-offspring transmission message passing

Detailed pedigree usage, embedded `father`/`mother` sample columns, equations, and validation benchmarks are documented in `docs/pedigree_tutorial.md`.
Detailed masked-CV calibration equations, no-call threshold behavior, benchmark interpretation, and future calibration scaling ideas are documented in `docs/CALIBRATION_MASKED_CV_REPORT.md`.

## Performance notes

The default pipeline settings are tuned for lower RAM and lower serialization cost:

- `read_mode="read_stream"` avoids pileup-column iteration overhead and processes aligned reads directly.
- `read_stream_backend="htslib"` (or `"auto"` when compiled extension is present) uses native HTSlib C++ traversal for the read-aware path.
- read extraction can be parallelized across samples (`io_workers > 1`) and each BAM can use HTSlib decompression threads (`htslib_threads_per_file > 1`).
- transition outputs are written in a compact form (`switch_probability`, `stay_probability`, `offdiag_probability`) by default rather than full state x state matrices.
- parquet writes use dictionary encoding plus `zstd` with configurable compression level.
- CPU runs default to a memory-efficient numpy forward-backward; GPU-capable hosts can run JAX (`hmm_backend="jax"` or `"auto"`).
- JAX diploid kernels support ahead-of-time lowering/compile-by-shape (`jax_aot_compile=True`) to reduce runtime JIT overhead.
- fragment likelihoods can be combined with per-site evidence (`fragment_likelihood_mode="augment"`) or replace centered per-site terms (`"replace"`).
- for stronger parity with original STITCH read-aware behavior, use `fragment_coupling_model="stitch_parity"` with rescaling controls (`fragment_max_diff_reads`, `fragment_max_emission_diff`).
- read matrices can be allocated as memory maps (`memory_map_read_matrices=True`) for lower peak RAM on large sample x SNP blocks.
- current implementation uses batched `pysam` streaming for read extraction.

To profile hotspots on a real dataset:

```bash
python benchmarks/profile_bottlenecks.py \
  --data-dir benchmark_runs/synth_5mb_2k_0p1x \
  --output-dir benchmark_runs/profile_new \
  --k 8 --iterations 2 --block-size 1000
```

To build the optional native HTSlib read-stream backend in-place:

```bash
python setup.py build_ext --inplace
```

Then use `read_stream_backend="htslib"` (or `"auto"`) in `PipelineConfig`.

To auto-tune backend and block size on a representative dataset:

```bash
python benchmarks/tune_backends.py \
  --data-dir benchmark_runs/synth_5mb_2k_0p1x \
  --output-dir benchmark_runs/tune_runs \
  --chromosome chrSynthetic \
  --k 8 --iterations 2 \
  --block-sizes 500,1000,2000 \
  --backends auto,numpy,jax \
  --fragment-modes replace,augment
```

To run repeated memory-growth checks:

```bash
python benchmarks/check_memory_leak.py \
  --data-dir benchmark_runs/synth_5mb_2k_0p1x \
  --output-dir benchmark_runs/memory_check \
  --chromosome chrSynthetic \
  --k 8 --iterations 2 --block-size 1000 \
  --backend auto --repeats 4
```

## Minimal usage

```python
import pandas as pd

from stitchv2 import PipelineConfig, StitchPipeline

samples = pd.DataFrame(
    {
        "sample_id": ["a", "b"],
        "bam_path": ["a.bam", "b.bam"],
        "generation": [10.0, 12.0],
    }
)

config = PipelineConfig(
    chromosome="chr1",
    positions_path="positions.parquet",
    output_dir="out",
    n_founders=8,
    block_size=5000,
    jax_sample_batch_size=64,  # lower peak JAX memory (0 = all samples)
    read_stream_backend="auto",
    io_workers=4,
    htslib_threads_per_file=2,
)

pipeline = StitchPipeline(config)
pipeline.prepare_inputs(samples)
```

## CLI

Run the pipeline:

```bash
stitchv2 run \
  --samples samples.parquet \
  --positions positions.parquet \
  --chromosome chr1 \
  --output-dir out \
  --n-founders 8 \
  --hmm-backend jax \
  --jax-sample-batch-size 64 \
  --read-stream-backend auto \
  --io-workers 8 \
  --htslib-threads-per-file 2 \
  --fragment-coupling-model stitch_parity \
  --write-genotype-posteriors \
  --write-genotype-calls \
  --write-support-mask \
  --microarray-plink /path/to/microarray_prefix \
  --memory-map-read-matrices
```

JAX-only benchmark scripts:

```bash
python benchmarks/benchmark_calibration_strategies.py \
  --data-dir benchmark_runs/synth_5mb_2k_0p1x \
  --run-dir benchmark_runs/readaware_matrix_v2_synth_jax_hap0 \
  --output-dir benchmark_runs/calibration_synth_jax \
  --use-optuna --optuna-trials 20

python benchmarks/benchmark_real_subsample_jax.py \
  --data-dir benchmark_runs/stitch_example \
  --output-dir benchmark_runs/real_subsample_jax \
  --chromosome 19 --k 8 --iterations 2

python benchmarks/generate_calibration_benchmark_report.py \
  --benchmark-dir benchmark_runs/calibration_synth_jax

python benchmarks/generate_real_subsample_benchmark_report.py \
  --benchmark-dir benchmark_runs/real_subsample_jax

python benchmarks/generate_readaware_v3_detailed_report.py
```

Profile/tune JAX memory and runtime:

```bash
stitchv2 tune-jax-memory \
  --data-dir benchmark_runs/synth_5mb_2k_0p1x \
  --output-dir benchmark_runs/tune_jax \
  --chromosome chrSynthetic \
  --n-founders 8 \
  --block-sizes 500,1000,2000 \
  --memory-map-modes off,on \
  --jax-sample-batch-sizes 0,64,128
```

Combine parquet outputs, optionally writing floating outputs to xarray/Zarr:

```bash
stitchv2 combine --run-output-dir out
stitchv2 combine --run-output-dir out --write-zarr --zarr-output out/combined/float_outputs.zarr
stitchv2 export-bcf --run-output-dir out --output-bcf out/stitch.chr1.bcf --chromosome chr1
```

BCF is only an explicit interoperability path and WILL slow down I/O. Keep primary results in Parquet/Zarr. STITCHV2 does not allow VCF export.

Run cross-validation/tuning (5-fold, 20% holdout, seed averaging):

```bash
stitchv2 cv \
  --samples samples.parquet \
  --positions positions.parquet \
  --pseudo-truth pseudo_truth.parquet \
  --chromosome chr1 \
  --output-dir out_cv \
  --k-values 6,8,10 \
  --ngen-values 0.75,1.0,1.25 \
  --s-values 2,3 \
  --seeds 0,1,2 \
  --folds 5 \
  --holdout-fraction 0.2 \
  --hmm-backend jax \
  --fragment-coupling-model stitch_parity \
  --lightgbm-post-calibrator
```

`combine_pipeline_outputs(...)` returns a lazy `xarray.Dataset` (dask-backed) and stores combination metadata in `dataset.attrs["combine_summary"]`.

## Tutorial Notebook

See [docs/stitchv2_tutorial.ipynb](docs/stitchv2_tutorial.ipynb), or the markdown companion [docs/stitchv2_tutorial.md](docs/stitchv2_tutorial.md), for an end-to-end walkthrough of:

- input file formats
- every `stitchv2 run` parameter with the closest original STITCH equivalent
- BAM/CRAM, PLINK, parquet, founder, microarray, and pedigree workflows
- mixed haploid/diploid and sex-chromosome ploidy handling
- resource scaling with samples, SNPs, founders, ploidy, JAX, and Dask
- block/chunk outputs and their schemas
- combining parquet chunks
- explicit BCF interoperability export and native Parquet/Zarr outputs for downstream conversion

For a parameter-by-parameter synthetic walkthrough (including mutable vs STITCH-parity founder settings), see:
